Target: OWASP Juice Shop — complaints functionality Severity: High CVSS: 8.1
Description
The complaints functionality trusts a client-controlled UserId rather than deriving the ownership relationship from the
authenticated session. Supplied evidence demonstrates both cross-user write and cross-user read behavior.
Impact
An authenticated low-privileged user can create or access complaint records in the context of another user. This
breaks user isolation and enables unauthorized disclosure and modification of complaint data.
Step-by-Step Reproduction
1. Authenticate as an ordinary Juice Shop customer.
2. Capture the complaints request in Burp.
3. Modify the client-controlled UserId while keeping the attacker session unchanged.
4. Replay the request and observe a complaint created for another user.
5. Issue the corresponding read request with another user’s identifier and observe that user’s complaint data.
6. Confirm the server does not enforce ownership from the authenticated principal.
Remediation
Derive ownership from the authenticated identity on the server. Authorize every complaint read/write against the
current principal and reject mismatched ownership. Never treat a client-supplied UserId as a trusted authorization
decision.
Tradecraft / Methodology
For BOLA testing, keep the session fixed and mutate only the object or ownership identifier. Test both read and write
operations because an API can incorrectly protect one while exposing the other.
